.TH UNCOMPRESS 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
uncompress \- decompress files made by compress
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uncompress
.RB [ \-cf ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uncompress
decompresses files in the format of the Unix
.B compress
program, which are conventionally named with the suffix
.BR .Z .
Such a file begins with the octets
.B 0x1F 0x9D
and holds LZW-coded data with up to 16 bits per code, with or without
block mode.
There is no
.B compress
applet, so busybox only reads this format.
.PP
Each
.I file
is replaced by its decompressed form.
The output is named after the input with the
.B .Z
suffix removed, and the input is deleted once the output is complete.
A name that does not end in
.B .Z
is an error and is skipped, with the message
.BR "unknown suffix \- ignored" .
The comparison is case-sensitive, and the suffix is never added to a name.
An existing output file is an error unless
.B \-f
is given.
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
the command reads standard input and writes standard output.
Each named file replaces standard input in turn, so a
.B \-
after a named file fails with
.BR "invalid magic" .
With
.BR \-c ,
all output goes to standard output, the input files are kept, and
compressed file names need not end in
.BR .Z .
.PP
When a file fails, the error is reported on standard error, the input
file is kept, the partial output file is removed, and the remaining
files are still processed.
Output is written in blocks of 2048 octets, and the block in progress
at the error is lost, so
.B \-c
writes only the complete blocks decoded before it.
.PP
Some failures end the command at once instead, and leave any partial
output file in place.
They are reading standard input from a console without
.BR \-f ,
a read or write error, failing to delete a file, and some kinds of
damaged data, such as a first code that is not a literal octet.
.SS Diagnostics
Messages on standard error begin with the command name and a colon.
.TP
.B invalid magic
The input does not begin with the octets
.BR "0x1F 0x9D" ,
or is shorter than that.
.TP
.B short read
The input ends after those two octets, before the octet that gives the
maximum code size.
.TP
.BI "compressed with " n " bits, can only handle 16 bits"
The file uses codes wider than 16 bits.
.TP
.B corrupted data
A code is not valid at its place in the stream.
.TP
.IB name ": unknown suffix \- ignored"
The name does not end in
.BR .Z .
.TP
.B compressed data not read from terminal, use \-f to force it
Standard input is a console and
.B \-f
was not given.
.PP
A file that cannot be read, created, or deleted is reported with the
system's reason, as in
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
uncompress: can\(aqt open \(aqnotes.txt\(aq: File exists
.EE
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-cf ,
and may follow file operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-c
Write the decompressed data of every file to standard output, in order,
and keep the input files.
No output file is created, so the names need not end in
.BR .Z .
.TP
.B \-f
Delete an existing output file, even a read-only one, before
decompression starts, instead of failing because it exists.
The old file is lost even if the input then proves to be invalid.
Also read standard input even when it is a console.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the command line are expanded at startup, for callers such
as
.B cmd.exe
that do not expand them.
The C runtime does the expansion, on quoted arguments too, and a pattern
that matches nothing stays as it is.
A busybox program started without this variable sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so the commands it runs do not expand their arguments again.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask applied at startup.
If it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 0200 ,
output files are created read-only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must precede the file operands.
.PP
The other
.B BB_
variables described in
.BR busybox (1)
apply to this command as to every applet.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid option, or an error with any file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decompress a file, replacing it with
.IR notes.txt :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uncompress notes.txt.Z
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decompress a file, keeping the original:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uncompress \-c notes.txt.Z >notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decompress a file, replacing an existing
.IR notes.txt :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uncompress \-f notes.txt.Z
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decompress a stream:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uncompress <notes.txt.Z >notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
List a compressed tar archive, with
.B uncompress
or with
.B tar
alone:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uncompress \-c src.tar.Z | tar \-tf \-
$ tar \-tf src.tar.Z
.EE
.RE
.PP
Skip a file that lacks the suffix:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uncompress README
uncompress: README: unknown suffix \- ignored
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the Unix
.BR uncompress :
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.B \-c
and
.B \-f
are accepted.
Other options, such as
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-v ,
and
.BR \-q ,
are errors that print the usage message on standard error.
.IP \(bu 2
A name must be given with its
.B .Z
suffix.
.B uncompress
does not try the name with the suffix added.
.IP \(bu 2
The output file gets the current time, not the input's modification
time.
.IP \(bu 2
The format has no checksum, so truncated input is not detected.
The part that decoded is written, the exit status is 0, and the input
file is deleted.
.PP
.B tar
reads
.B .Z
archives when given
.BR \-Z ,
or when it recognizes one by its contents, which works for a file but
not a pipe.
It decompresses them by running
.B uncompress \-cf \-
as a child process.
.PP
Standard input and output are in binary mode.
Output names are formed from the input path as written, so drive
letters and backslashes work, and the suffix test looks only at the text
after the last period.
A read-only input file is still deleted, and its output is also
read-only.
Paths may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR tar (1)
